# linux/deploy-manifest — the single truth about what lives where in a home on
# a Linux session host. Format: source target mode kind  (source relative to the
# repo root, target relative to the home root). One source may have several
# target rows. The row list was MEASURED against real homes — change it by
# measuring, not by guessing.
#
# An estate adds its own rows in its own manifest; the deploy composes the union
# and prints how many rows each side contributed. A stranger who clones this
# repo gets the mechanism's rows and nothing else.
#
# FILE NAMES ARE STILL BEING RENAMED, and one of them is now done. The session
# and job units used to be named after the estate; as of 2026-08-20 they carry a
# FUNCTION name instead — `agent-`, which belongs to neither the estate nor this
# product. That choice was deliberate: naming them after the product would have
# put the product's name into somebody's operational layer, and the next rename
# would drag thirty live timers along with it.
#
# The rename is only half an operation. New unit files can be DEPLOYED, but
# switching a host over means disabling the old instances and enabling the new
# ones per home — a service verb the deploy path refuses by design (see
# test/deploy-policy.test.sh: daemon-reload and nothing else). Until a host has
# had that done by hand, it runs the old units; the rows below describe what the
# deploy WRITES, never what a host currently runs.
#
# What remains named in the estate's own language: a directory and two scripts,
# all Swedish rather than estate-specific, and none of them load-bearing for a
# running unit. Those are a rename with no operational half.

# scripts
linux/session-supervisor-linux.sh  scripts/session-supervisor-linux.sh  755  scripts
runtime/opencode-session.sh  scripts/runtime/opencode-session.sh  755  scripts
linux/session-new.sh                scripts/session-new.sh                755  scripts
linux/session-approve.sh           scripts/session-approve.sh           755  scripts
linux/estate-status.sh             scripts/estate-status.sh             755  scripts
linux/liveness-host.sh             scripts/liveness-host.sh             755  scripts
job-runner.sh                      scripts/job-runner.sh                755  scripts
lib/registry.sh                    scripts/lib/registry.sh              755  lib
# THE SPAWN LIBRARIES TRAVEL WITH THE SUPERVISOR, and both of them or neither.
# The supervisor refuses to start a session when either is absent (rc 78) --
# a deployed supervisor that could not read its spawn policy would otherwise
# fall back to the legacy command line and hand every session whatever its
# checkout happens to declare, which is the one outcome the policy exists to
# prevent. Same directory as registry.sh, because that is where the supervisor
# looks: it sources these from the directory it found registry.sh in.
lib/mcprender.sh                   scripts/lib/mcprender.sh             755  lib
lib/mcpspawn.sh                    scripts/lib/mcpspawn.sh              755  lib
# bus clients + browser (executable locations — the loneliness sweep's core targets)
linux/bus-send                     bin/bus-send                         755  bin
linux/bus-read                     bin/bus-read                         755  bin
linux/browser-stack.sh             bin/browser-stack.sh                 755  bin
# THE WRAPPER A RENDERED mcp-config NAMES. The document writes the literal
# string ~/bin/mcp-env as an asset's command whenever that asset declares an
# MCP_ENV_FILE, so this row is what makes the path in the document real.
linux/mcp-env                      bin/mcp-env                          755  bin
# systemd templates (daemon-reload only; never enable/restart)
linux/agent-session@.service      .config/systemd/user/agent-session@.service  644  systemd
linux/agent-session@.timer        .config/systemd/user/agent-session@.timer    644  systemd
linux/agent-job@.service          .config/systemd/user/agent-job@.service      644  systemd
linux/browser-stack.service        .config/systemd/user/browser-stack.service    644  systemd
linux/browser-stack.timer          .config/systemd/user/browser-stack.timer      644  systemd
# The hub guard's self-refusing client — NOT a duplicate but a guard. A session
# owner's account once ran a working hub client where a refusing one belonged,
# and the mistake was invisible because the file looked correct. As a manifest
# target it is spared by the loneliness sweep AND measured by the drift gate: if
# anyone swaps the guard for a working hub client, the gate fails. The guard is
# guarded.
linux/hub/bus-send             scripts/bus/bus-send                 755  scripts
linux/hub/lib.sh               scripts/bus/lib.sh                   644  scripts
linux/hub/enroll               scripts/bus/enroll                   755  scripts
# The hub's receiving side. bus-relay-in is the forced command behind every
# session host's relay key in the hub's authorized_keys; bus-relay-deliver is the
# forced command behind the hub's delivery key in a neighbour's home on the same
# host. Both belong to the hub library above and land beside it, under bin/.
linux/hub/bin/bus-relay-in       scripts/bus/bin/bus-relay-in         755  scripts
linux/hub/bin/bus-relay-deliver  scripts/bus/bin/bus-relay-deliver    755  scripts
# The mechanical answerer for FRAGA. Its catalogue is the ESTATE'S file, delivered
# by the estate's own deploy beside the library; this file only runs what the
# catalogue's allow line permits.
linux/hub/bin/bus-fraga-svar     scripts/bus/bin/bus-fraga-svar       755  scripts
# The session watch: the hub's five-minute cycle over every session it operates.
# Pure decisions in lib.mjs, the estate read through registry-dump (the one
# reader), alarms through send-mail.mjs. Lands beside the bus and the library.
watch/lib.mjs                    scripts/watch/lib.mjs                644  scripts
watch/pane-rules.json            scripts/watch/pane-rules.json        644  scripts
watch/estate.mjs                 scripts/watch/estate.mjs             644  scripts
watch/send-mail.mjs              scripts/watch/send-mail.mjs          644  scripts
watch/resume.mjs                 scripts/watch/resume.mjs             644  scripts
watch/session-watch.mjs          scripts/watch/session-watch.mjs      644  scripts
watch/restart-session.mjs        scripts/watch/restart-session.mjs    644  scripts
watch/package.json               scripts/watch/package.json           644  scripts
watch/bin/registry-dump          scripts/watch/bin/registry-dump      755  scripts
watch/bin/session-watch.sh       scripts/watch/bin/session-watch.sh   755  scripts
watch/bin/restart-session.sh     scripts/watch/bin/restart-session.sh 755  scripts
